From e2aea1a24ad2385a510cad933182845305aae542 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 13:28:25 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] firewall: a rebuild no longer fights itself, and a rule can
 no longer vanish silently

One restart_qos;restart_firewall on the owner's RT-BE96U (2026-09-17 07:34) had
the three Reaper layer applies, hook.sh, rwatch 3c and an out-of-tree DNS
carve-out watcher contending for FORWARD position 1 for fifty seconds; the
Gatekeeper-restricted devices lost DNS four times. The same day's r4 boot lost
two of 126 Gatekeeper rules to a concurrent iptables writer (no xtables lock,
unchecked adds) and an internet-only device had no internet.

- gatekeeper: RETURN DNS the router itself redirected (--ctstate DNAT
  --ctorigdstport 53) ahead of the per-network DROP, so an off-segment
  resolver behind a Service Intercept needs no carve-out in front of the hook
- reaper_hook: hook.sh computes WHERE to pin with rwatch 3d's classifier
  (_front_pos) instead of -I 1; declared/narrow/terminal rules keep their place
- rwarden: sets created and the cache replayed BEFORE the chain teardown
  (the chain was absent for the whole replay); replay is one awk pass into
  per-set files instead of one pass per set (17 s measured on 80 sets);
  heal.sh re-checks under the firewall lock before re-applying
- rwatch 3c: a missing Warden chain must hold on two ticks, the heal runs
  under the lock, and FAIL (with the dump) only if it is still missing after
- reaper_fw + rdnshc: an intercept aimed at the DNS Health Check target goes
  into REAPER_FWHC, closed/opened by hcgate.sh on FAILOVER/RESTORED so it
  fails open; the gate script is written for apply, candidate and rollback
- gatekeeper + rwarden: every iptables/ip6tables/ebtables add is retried once
  and counted (failcount); gkd re-applies on a non-zero count (heal_lost_rules)

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
